        "text": "{\"scores\":{\"center\":5,\"craft\":4,\"facts\":5,\"age_fit\":5,\"continuity\":5,\"delight\":4},\"evidence\":{\"center\":\"Lennon acts in every chapter. He whacks the fading Tennis for Two dot, dodges torpedoes in Spacewar!, holds Ralph Baer's overlay, organises the arcade kids ('I need two Space Invaders experts and two Pac-Man pros!') and builds the finale in Roblox Studio with 'a goal. Then a penalty spot.' His Roblox and building likes drive the climax, and soccer gets a nod. The thinner chapters still give him distinct lines ('No idea what you mean', 'Wait wait wait. Are these the...').\",\"craft\":\"The story has a strong cold open in Roblox Studio, a clear device (the green cursor and the dial-up 'sneeze'), a coin-collecting shape and a return home with a callback message. Levels 6–8 drop the deletion-rescue beat, so the middle becomes a tour. The climactic thesis is also muddled: Lennon says levels were saved because 'somebody made something new', yet he saved them by playing.\",\"facts\":\"The dates, people and places are correct and woven into scenes: Higinbotham at Brookhaven in 1958, Spacewar! at MIT, Baer's overlays, Andy Capp's quarters, Jumpman, the E.T. burial dug up in 2014, Tetris on the 1989 Game Boy, DynaBlocks in 2004 and launch in 2006, and Builderman. The legends are hedged: the Japan coin shortage is 'might be a bit of an exaggeration' and the pizza origin is 'He said'. The True Facts list matches the story.\",\"age_fit\":\"At 2,471 words the story is within range. The dry, self-aware humour suits a 10-year-old ('Honestly? Respect', 'Multiplayer,' corrected Lennon. 'Obviously.'). Big words like oscilloscope are explained in the text. Peril is moderate and never frightening.\",\"continuity\":\"This is the first story with no bible. It is internally consistent: the coin count runs correctly to seven, and Bit and Builderman remain stable.\",\"delight\":\"Many lines land for both parent and child: 'a dial-up modem having a sneeze